   ccuurrssccrr,, ssttddssccrr,, nneewwssccrr
       The  library  records  updates to the terminal screen in a window named
       ccuurrssccrr.  This object  is  referred  to  as  the  “physical  screen”  in
       ccuurrss__rreeffrreesshh(3X) and ccuurrss__oouuttooppttss(3X).

       _n_c_u_r_s_e_s  collects  pending  updates  to the terminal screen in a window
       named nneewwssccrr.  This object is referred to as the  “virtual  screen”  in
       the  ccuurrss__kkeerrnneell(3X), ccuurrss__rreeffrreesshh(3X), and ccuurrss__oouuttooppttss(3X).  When the
       screen is refreshed, _c_u_r_s_e_s determines a minimal set of  updates  using
       the terminal's capabilities to make ccuurrssccrr look like nneewwssccrr.

       Once  _c_u_r_s_e_s  is  initialized, it creates a window named ssttddssccrr.  It is
       the same size as the terminal screen and is the default window used  by
       routines  that  do  not  take a parameter identifying one.  Many _c_u_r_s_e_s
       functions use this window.

   CCOOLLOORRSS
       Once _c_u_r_s_e_s is initialized, CCOOLLOORRSS contains the number of  colors  sup‐
       ported by the terminal; see ccuurrss__ccoolloorr(3X).

   CCOOLLOORR__PPAAIIRRSS
       Once  _c_u_r_s_e_s  is  initialized, CCOOLLOORR__PPAAIIRRSS contains the number of color
       pairs supported by the terminal; see ccuurrss__ccoolloorr(3X).

   CCOOLLSS,, LLIINNEESS
       Once _c_u_r_s_e_s is initialized, CCOOLLSS and LLIINNEESS contain the  screen's  width
       and  height  in  character  cells, respectively; that is, the number of
       columns and lines.

   EESSCCDDEELLAAYY
       For _c_u_r_s_e_s to distinguish the ESC character  resulting  from  a  user's
       press of the “Escape” key on the input device from one beginning an _e_s_‐
       _c_a_p_e _s_e_q_u_e_n_c_e (as commonly produced by function keys), it  waits  after
       the  escape character to see if further characters are available on the
       input stream within a short interval.  EESSCCDDEELLAAYY stores this interval in
       milliseconds.

       If kkeeyyppaadd(3X) is disabled for the _c_u_r_s_e_s window receiving input, a pro‐
       gram must disambiguate escape sequences itself.

