PPOORRTTAABBIILLIITTYY
       The X/Open Curses standard documents all of  the  foregoing  types  and
       symbols except for nneewwssccrr, TTAABBSSIIZZEE, and EESSCCDDEELLAAYY.

       X/Open  Curses  describes  ccuurrssccrr only as “an internal data structure”;
       SVr4 gave more details, noting its use “for  certain  low-level  opera‐
       tions  like  clearing and redrawing a screen containing garbage”.  Nei‐
       ther specified its interaction with the rest of  the  interface  beyond
       use as an argument to cclleeaarrookk(3X) and wwrreeffrreesshh(3X).

       nneewwssccrr  is a feature of SVr4 _c_u_r_s_e_s.  When refreshing the screen, it is
       used as a working area for combining the standard  window  ssttddssccrr  with
       any  others the application may have created with nneewwwwiinn(3X).  When the
       update of nneewwssccrr is complete, _c_u_r_s_e_s modifies ccuurrssccrr to match nneewwssccrr.

       TTAABBSSIIZZEE is a feature of SVr4 _c_u_r_s_e_s.

       •   SVr4  initially  sets  TTAABBSSIIZZEE  from  the  terminal   description's
           iinniitt__ttaabbss  capability.   After  that, it can be altered by applica‐
           tions using SVr4 _c_u_r_s_e_s.

       •   SVr4 _c_u_r_s_e_s uses the value of TTAABBSSIIZZEE to compute  the  position  of
           tab  stops when updating both the virtual screen with aaddddcchh(3X) and
           the physical screen with mmvvccuurr(3X).

       •   _n_c_u_r_s_e_s uses the value  of  TTAABBSSIIZZEE  only  to  update  the  virtual
           screen.   It uses the terminal description's “iitt” (iinniitt__ttaabbss) capa‐
           bility for computing hardware tabs (that is, tab stops on the phys‐
           ical screen).

       •   Other  implementations  differ.  For instance, NetBSD _c_u_r_s_e_s allows
           TTAABBSSIIZZEE to be set through an environment  variable.   _n_c_u_r_s_e_s  does
           not.

           NetBSD _c_u_r_s_e_s does not support hardware tabs; it uses the iinniitt__ttaabbss
           capability and the TTAABBSSIIZZEE variable only for updating  the  virtual
           screen.

       EESSCCDDEELLAAYY is a feature of AIX _c_u_r_s_e_s.

       •   In AIX, the units for EESSCCDDEELLAAYY are _f_i_f_t_h_s of milliseconds.

       •   The default value for AIX's EESSCCDDEELLAAYY equals 0.1 seconds.

       •   AIX  also  enforces a limit of 10,000 seconds for EESSCCDDEELLAAYY; _n_c_u_r_s_e_s
           does not enforce any upper limit.

       _n_c_u_r_s_e_s has long used EESSCCDDEELLAAYY with units of  milliseconds,  making  it
       impossible  to  be  completely compatible with AIX.  Consequently, most
       users have decided either to override the value, or to  rely  upon  its
       default.

